  "text": "**Review of *The Great Gatsby* by F. Scott Fitzgerald**\n\nF. Scott Fitzgerald\u2019s *The Great Gatsby* is a timeless masterpiece that captures the essence of the American Dream and its often tragic consequences. Set in the roaring 1920s, the novel is a poignant exploration of love, ambition, and the illusion of the past. Through the eyes of Nick Carraway, the story unfolds in the glittering world of Long Island, where the lives of the wealthy and the yearning of the dreamer collide.\n\nThe narrative centers on Jay Gatsby, a mysterious billionaire whose grand estate, West Egg, is a symbol of his desire to reclaim the past and win back Daisy Buchanan, his lost love. Gatsby\u2019s parties, opulence, and relentless pursuit of the American Dream contrast sharply with the moral decay and social stratification of the time. The novel\u2019s critique of the wealthy elite is embodied in characters like Tom Buchanan, whose arrogance and entitlement reflect the corruption of the upper class.\n\nThe setting of East Egg and West Egg serves as a metaphor for the divide between old money and new money. Whereas East Egg represents inherited wealth and social status, West Egg symbolizes the self-made success of the 1920s. The Valley of Ashes, a desolate area between the two Eggs, echoes the moral and social decay that lies beneath the surface of the glittering parties and illusions.\n\nOne of the most powerful elements of the novel is its tragic ending, which underscores the futility of Gatsby\u2019s dream. His death, caused by a car accident, and the indifference of the wealthy to his fate reveal the hollowness of the American Dream. Nick\u2019s final reflection that \u201cGatsby believed in the green light, the orgastic future that year by year recedes before us\u201d is a haunting reminder of the elusiveness of hope and the passage of time.\n\nThe characters in *The Great Gatsby* are deeply drawn and complex. Daisy Buchanan, though flawed, represents the unattainable ideal of beauty and love. Her husband, Tom, is a symbol of the oppressive traditions of old money. Jordan Baker, with her wit and independence, adds another layer to the narrative, particularly in her relationship with Gatsby.\n\nFitzgerald\u2019s lyrical prose and rich symbolism set *The Great Gatsby* apart as a literary classic. The recurring green light at the end of Daisy\u2019s dock, the eyes of Dr. T.J. Eckleburg, and the recurring motif of the American Dream all contribute to the novel\u2019s enduring power.\n\nIn conclusion, *The Great Gatsby* is not just a story of one man\u2019s dream, but a reflection on the values and illusions of an entire era. Its themes of love, desire, and the American Dream continue to resonate with readers today, making it a must-read for anyone interested in the complexities of human nature and the pursuit of happiness.\n\n**Proper Nouns from the Book in the Review:**\n\n1. The Great Gatsby  \n2. F. Scott Fitzgerald  \n3. Nick Carraway  \n4. Jay Gatsby  \n5. West Egg  \n6. Long Island  \n7. Daisy Buchanan  \n8. Tom Buchanan  \n9. East Egg  \n10. Valley of Ashes  \n11. Dr. T.J. Eckleburg  \n12. American Dream  \n13. Jordan Baker  \n\n**Total Proper Nouns: 13**",
